proof: aaru | fedora | node-floppy
date: 2026-07-15
lane/VM: test-fedora (Fedora 44 KDE), repo COPR gmipf/media-preservation
user groups: "gmipf wheel" (NO cdrom/disk/plugdev) -> groupless
device: NEC USB UF000x floppy (USB-UFI 0409:0040) -> /dev/sda (1.4M, media present)
mechanism: packaged udev rule /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-aaru-floppy.rules
  (SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_TYPE}=="floppy", TAG+="uaccess"), applied by
  systemd 73-seat-late.rules uaccess builtin; NO cap needed (plain read()).

Note: floppy is NOT ID_CDROM, so systemd's own 70-uaccess.rules does NOT grant it
(verified: 70-uaccess.rules has no floppy clause). Our rule is the sole source.
Note: uaccess tag is sticky in the udev db, so RED/GREEN needs a FRESH device
enumeration (USB detach/attach), not just udevadm trigger. ACL arrives ~2s after
hotplug (poll, don't check once).

--- RED (no floppy rule, fresh enumeration) ---
$ (all 70-*-floppy.rules removed) ; virsh detach+attach usb-floppy ; fresh /dev/sda
  CURRENT_TAGS=:systemd:        # no uaccess
  getfacl: no user:gmipf entry  # ACL ABSENT
  dd if=/dev/sda bs=512 count=1 -> "Keine Berechtigung" (Permission denied)

--- GREEN (ONLY 70-aaru-floppy.rules present, fresh enumeration) ---
$ (only aaru rule) ; virsh detach+attach usb-floppy ; fresh /dev/sda
  udevadm test: ID_TYPE=floppy ; TAGS=:uaccess:seat:systemd:
  ACL present @~2s: user:gmipf:rw-
  CURRENT_TAGS=:systemd:seat:uaccess:
  dd if=/dev/sda bs=512 count=1 -> OK

VERDICT: PROVEN. aaru's packaged 70-aaru-floppy.rules alone grants the floppy node
uaccess ACL to a groupless user on a fresh plug; without any floppy rule the same
fresh device is denied. Control RED (no rule) reproduced denied at end of run.
